---
// Landing for people who maintain WordPress sites and dread plugin updates. Facts from
// documentation/src/content/docs/ci/plugin-update-prs.md and cli/src/commands/plugin/
// (update.ts, audit.ts, add.ts, push.ts).
import DocPage from "@/components/site/DocPage.astro";

export const prerender = true;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: "Loopress", url: "https://loopress.dev/" },
  { name: "Plugin updates", url: "https://loopress.dev/plugin-updates" },
];
---

<DocPage title="Test WordPress Plugin Updates Before Production" description="Pin plugin versions in Git, bump them with one command, and let a weekly CI job install the new versions on a throwaway WordPress and open a pull request." kicker="plugin updates · wordpress · git" heading="A plugin update is a pull request, not a click on production." lede="WooCommerce updates itself, the checkout breaks, and nobody can say what changed. Pin the versions in Git and the update becomes a diff you review before it reaches a client site." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <h2>Versions are pinned in the repo</h2>
  <p>
    Plugins are listed with their version in <code>loopress.json</code>. Every environment installs
    exactly that version, and an update is a one-line diff. This applies to projects without a
    <code>composer.json</code>: when one exists, <code>lps plugin push</code> uses Composer's versions and
    <code>lps plugin status</code> does not compare these pins, so update plugins through Composer instead.
  </p>
  <pre>$ lps plugin update --minor
$ git diff loopress.json
-    "woocommerce": "9.4.2",
+    "woocommerce": "9.5.0",</pre>
  <p>
    <code>lps plugin update</code> rewrites <code>loopress.json</code> only. Nothing changes on a site
    until <code>lps plugin push</code>. <code>--minor</code> stays on the current major version,
    <code>--patch</code> takes patch releases only, and <code>lps plugin update woocommerce</code> bumps
    one plugin.
  </p>

  <h2>A weekly pull request, from CI</h2>
  <p>
    A GitHub Actions workflow runs <code>lps plugin update</code> every Monday, installs the new
    versions on a throwaway WordPress booted inside the job, runs the audit, and opens a pull request
    with a before and after table. If a version does not install, the job fails and no pull request is
    opened.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add each plugin with an exact version (<code>lps plugin add woocommerce --version 9.4.2</code>): the default <code>latest</code> is never updated, so the workflow would report no change. It is a workflow you copy into your repository, not a hosted service: no token, no account, and none
    of your real sites is touched. It installs on a throwaway WordPress, so it tells you a version
    installs, not that your site still works: you still review the pull request. <a href="https://docs.loopress.dev/ci/plugin-update-prs/">Get the workflow file</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Audit the versions you pin</h2>
  <pre>$ lps plugin audit
$ lps plugin status --env staging</pre>
  <p>
    <code>lps plugin audit</code> checks every pinned version against the <a href="https://www.wpvulnerability.net/">wpvulnerability.net</a> database and exits non-zero when a
    known vulnerability affects one of them. <code>lps plugin status</code> shows how far a site is
    from the pins.
  </p>

  <h2>When an update goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Pin the previous version back and push. A downgrade needs <code>--force</code>: the old files come
    back, but the database migrations the new version ran do not. Read the changelog first.
  </p>
  <pre>$ lps plugin add woocommerce --version 9.4.2
$ lps plugin push --env production --force</pre>

  <div class="ctas">
    <a href="https://docs.loopress.dev/ci/plugin-update-prs/" class="btn">Get the workflow</a>
    <a href="https://docs.loopress.dev/getting-started/">Get started</a>
    <a href="/">See the whole loop</a>
  </div>
  <p class="trust">free and open source · WordPress 6.2+, PHP 8.2+ · no SSH, no host change · <a href="/changelog">alpha, moving fast</a></p>
</DocPage>

